{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Two MORE failures from the full tier, both mine, both fixed. Recording because the third one was a genuine design trap I would not have found by reasoning.\n\n2. tests/unit/test_workflow_names.py::test_every_workflow_is_named_in_the_locked_table — the repo locks every workflow filename to an exact display name, and a new workflow with no entry fails until it is named deliberately. Registered compact-sweep.yml -> 'Compaction Sweep'. Working exactly as intended; nothing to argue with.\n\n3. tests/interfaces/store/test_inline_write_push.py::test_compact_all_keeps_the_legacy_best_effort_push_contract — this one is the interesting one. It asserts compact-all always triggers push_after_commit. It broke because of my HONEST COUNTING change, not my selection change, and the coupling was invisible:\n\n  - The old code counted every rc==0 as compacted, including folds that wrote nothing. So 'compacted > 0' really meant 'we selected something', and the commit+push tail behind it effectively always ran.\n  - Once counting became honest, a sweep that folds nothing has compacted == 0, so the tail — and with it the push — was silently skipped.\n\nThat is a real regression in a contract that has nothing to do with counting: the push exists so an earlier unpushed write in the same session is not stranded, and a sweep is often the last thing to run. Whether THIS sweep folded anything is irrelevant to it.\n\nFixed by decoupling them. A best-effort push now runs on every non-dry-run invocation that commits nothing — including the 'Nothing to do' early return, which previously could not push at all — while the commit+push tail still runs when something was actually folded. --no-commit still opts out of both, as before.\n\nNote this test also exposed why I keep the backfill arm's 'at least one foldable event' condition: its fixture ticket is freshly created, so every event is inside the 30-minute horizon and nothing is foldable yet. The sweep correctly does nothing and correctly still pushes. The next scheduled run, 6 hours later, sees aged events and folds them.\n\nTally for this story: the full tier caught 3 failures, all mine, none of which the unit tier or my own reasoning caught — a selection regression, a config-lock omission, and a coupling between an honest-counting change and an unrelated push contract. Re-running both tiers now with all three fixed; gates already re-verified clean (lint, mypy 519 files, complexity baseline unchanged, generators zero diff)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786608375645419001,"uuid":"8f7c8071-3e42-4c49-81d6-c18e970d8d68"}